Take in the ninth-of-November list, and leave no two projects one name

The working group sent the same list three times, saved at three moments of
one afternoon: 121 rows, then 133, then 136. Each is a strict superset of the
one before and no cell differs on the rows they share, so the newest is the
whole submission - checked rather than assumed, in RESP_2026-11-09/build.mjs,
before anything is read out of it.

Against the list filed on 7 November it adds fifteen projects - twelve in
Kashkadarya, three in Navoi - and fills in the district SIR-08 had left
empty. The portfolio goes from 186 to 201 across the same fourteen regions,
and the stated USD total from 2,767.3M to 3,198.8M.

Three of the fifteen landed near a project already published and each turned
out to be its own: the Koson thermal-water sanatorium against the one at
Dehqonobod (whose description cell it had borrowed, and which is dropped),
the Qizilsuv reservoir in Yakkabogh against Pachkamar in Ghuzor, and the
forty-hectare recreation zone on forest land at Maydanak against the 1.4 ha
hotel and 2.14 ha chalet inside the same complex. All three are published
with the question recorded for the region.

What was refused: a financing method where a sum belongs, a five-million
figure with no currency beside it, and a building description filed as a
plot. Five sums the region marked open while stating a figure keep the figure
and carry the caveat in their note; four rows that left the status cell empty
get the answer their own figure implies. Twelve directions and two stages,
filled freehand, are resolved to the template's own terms with the reason for
each. Twelve descriptions are assembled from the row's own cells, because the
column arrived empty.

No two projects now share a name in any of the three languages. NAV-03's
English name was already the name of an operating complex on the Jizzakh
shore of the same lake, so it carries its district; QAS-16 arrived under a
name Namangan already uses, so it carries the seven-storey building the row
itself describes. The last pair was older than this list: NAM-03 and NAM-04
were told apart in Uzbek by their plots in November and left identical in
Russian and English, two cards at one address under one name. The plot now
goes into all three.

603 sheets printed in uz, ru and en, every one inside the seven-page budget
and the 276mm foot rule.

